20:26:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right, and I think I see your point more clearly now: **the mere existence of redactions isn’t the problem—it’s whether an agency can simply refuse to give you anything at all.** And you’re highlighting a crucial obligation in FOIA that often gets overlooked: the government has to actually *respond* and must give you whatever it can, even if it’s just fragments. That is, in fact, a core part of the statute. FOIA doesn’t just require disclosure; it requires a *process*: - Agencies **must** conduct a reasonable search for records. - They **must** determine what can be released and what falls under an exemption. - They **must** release **any reasonably segregable portion** of a record after redacting the exempt material. They can’t simply withhold an entire document because a few paragraphs are sensitive (unless the exempt and non-exempt parts are so intertwined that releasing the remainder would be meaningless or still reveal the protected information). - And they **must** provide a written response that, if they are denying information, cites the specific exemption(s) and how they apply. So, to your point: **they can’t just refuse to disclose anything at all without justification, and they can’t ignore you.** If an agency says “no records” or “we’re withholding everything,” it has to explain *why* (for example, “the entire record is classified at the Top Secret level and no portion can be segregated” or “no responsive records were found”). ### But what about when they really *do* refuse to disclose anything? There are a few scenarios where you might get essentially a blank wall: 1. **No responsive records** – The agency says it searched and found nothing. That’s a complete non-disclosure, but it’s still a response, and you can challenge the adequacy of the search. 2. **Full denial under an exemption** – If a document is *entirely* exempt and no meaningful information can be segregated, the agency can withhold the whole thing. You’d get a letter saying, “This record is withheld in full under Exemption 1 (classified).” That’s still a transparent declaration—you know what they’re withholding and why, and you can appeal or sue. 3. **The “Glomar” response** – In some cases, the agency may refuse to even **confirm or deny the existence** of records if acknowledging that they exist would itself cause the harm the exemption is designed to prevent (classic example: “we can neither confirm nor deny the existence of records on that covert operation”). That’s the closest thing to a total refusal, but it too must be justified under a specific exemption and is reviewable in court. These are all still legally constrained processes. They aren’t “refusals to engage”—they are, by design, part of the accountability structure. And crucially, **if the agency improperly withholds everything (or fails to segregate), a judge can order it to re-process and release.** ### Why this is still consistent with “freedom of information” The concept isn’t “you get everything, no exceptions.” It’s “you have a **right to ask**, and the government has a **duty to justify** any withholding, with a strong default in favor of disclosure.” The right to *know what you aren’t getting and why*—and to challenge that in court—is a huge part of what makes the system accountable. That’s a world away from a government that simply ignores requests or hands out blank pages with no explanation. So your instinct is spot on. FOIA’s power is not just in the bits they release, but in the **obligation to respond and to maximize what they can give you**, even when large swaths must be redacted. When an agency truly gives you nothing, it still has to show its work, and that’s a safeguard in itself.
