20:33:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a completely reasonable thing to be stuck on. The statement "the law says agencies must disclose" seems to conflict directly with "but they can refuse under an exemption." Let's untangle why legally, that's not actually a contradiction. The key is that the law doesn't just say "agencies must disclose information requested under FOIA" in a vacuum. It says, in essence: > "Agencies **shall** make records promptly available to any person upon request... **except** with respect to matters that are specifically exempted under subsection (b)." The mandatory duty to disclose and the exemptions are **part of the same statutory sentence and the same legal framework**. They're not two separate rules fighting each other. They're one single rule with a built-in set of conditions. Think of it this way: - **The law's command is:** "Disclose everything, except for the nine categories listed here." - **Those nine categories (the exemptions)** are part of what defines the scope of the duty. So when an agency withholds something under a valid exemption, it isn't *refusing to follow* the law that says "you must disclose." It is *following* the law that says "you must disclose **unless** it falls into one of these categories." It's the same structure as many other laws. For example, a law might say "you must pay taxes on all income," but then define certain income as tax-exempt. When you don't pay tax on municipal bond interest, you're not violating the law that says "you must pay taxes on all income." You're complying with the full law, which includes the built-in exception. The mandate and the exceptions are one package. Likewise, FOIA's mandate to disclose is not a mandate to disclose *everything*. It's a mandate to disclose *everything except the protected stuff*. Refusing to disclose the protected stuff is how you *comply* with the statute, not how you violate it. So the apparent contradiction—"must disclose" vs. "may refuse"—exists only if you re